## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design efficient dynamic ranking systems with batched updates, requiring you to balance update locality against query efficiency in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Ordered maps for score-based organization
- Lazy maximum score tracking to avoid unnecessary recomputation
- Efficient top-K extraction through bucket traversal
- Amortized time complexity analysis
- Memory usage optimization techniques

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode implementation
class TopKKeeper {
  constructor(users) {
    this.scoreToIds = new TreeMap(); // score -> TreeSet of user IDs
    this.userToScore = new HashMap(); // user ID -> current score
    this.maxScore = null;

    // Initialize in O(n)
    for (const [id, score] of users) {
      this.addUserToBucket(id, score);
    }
  }

  updateBatch(updates) {
    // Process B updates in O(B log n)
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const oldScore = this.userToScore.get(id);
      const newScore = oldScore + delta;

      this.removeUserFromBucket(id, oldScore);
      this.addUserToBucket(id, newScore);
    }
  }

  getTopK(k) {
    // Extract top-K in O(K log n)
    const result = [];
    let currentScore = this.maxScore;
    let remaining = k;

    while (remaining > 0 && currentScore !== null) {
      const ids = this.scoreToIds.get(currentScore);
      const takeCount = Math.min(remaining, ids.size());

      // Extract first 'takeCount' IDs (already sorted)
      result.push(...Array.from(ids).slice(0, takeCount));
      remaining -= takeCount;
      currentScore = this.scoreToIds.lowerKey(currentScore);
    }

    return result.slice(0, k);
  }
}
```

## Optimizations
1. **Lazy Maximum Updates**: Only recompute the maximum score when the current maximum bucket becomes empty, avoiding O(log n) overhead on every update.

2. **Batch Top-K Extraction**: Cache the current top-K result and only recompute when updates affect users in the top-K, reducing query frequency from every batch to only when necessary.

## Comparison to Rebuilding
Rebuilding from scratch would require O(n) time per batch to sort all users, resulting in O(n × batches) total time. Our approach achieves O(B log n) updates and O(K log n) queries, providing significant improvement when K << n and B is moderate.

## Complexity Analysis
- **Initialization**: O(n) - one insertion per user
- **Batch Updates**: O(B log n) - each update requires remove/insert operations
- **Top-K Query**: O(K log n) - traverse at most K score buckets
- **Space**: O(n) - store user-score mappings and score buckets

## Why This Is Asked
Designing dynamic SSSP algorithms is essential for real-time network applications. This question tests understanding of worst-case versus amortized guarantees, appropriate data structure selection, and practical implementation constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic shortest paths with nonnegative edge weights
- Localized re-relaxation and bounded Dijkstra execution
- Amortized analysis and worst-case performance bounds
- Memory-efficient data structures and practical optimizations
- Hierarchical update strategies for different edge types

## Code Example
```javascript
// Dynamic SSSP maintenance implementation
class DynamicSSSP {
  constructor(graph, source) {
    this.graph = graph;
    this.source = source;
    this.distances = new Array(graph.N).fill(Infinity);
    this.parents = new Array(graph.N).fill(-1);
    this.heap = new FibonacciHeap();
    this.affectedNodes = new Set();

    // Initialize with Dijkstra
    this.initializeSSSP();
  }

  updateEdge(u, v, newWeight) {
    const oldWeight = this.graph.getWeight(u, v);
    this.graph.setWeight(u, v, newWeight);

    if (newWeight < oldWeight) {
      this.handleWeightDecrease(u, v, newWeight);
    } else {
      this.handleWeightIncrease(u, v, newWeight);
    }
  }

  handleWeightDecrease(u, v, newWeight) {
    if (this.parents[v] === u) {
      // Tree edge weight decreased
      this.propagateDecrease(v);
    } else if (this.distances[u] + newWeight < this.distances[v]) {
      // Non-tree edge creates shorter path
      this.updateDistance(v, this.distances[u] + newWeight, u);
    }
  }

  handleWeightIncrease(u, v, newWeight) {
    if (this.parents[v] === u) {
      // Tree edge weight increased - need re-relaxation
      this.affectedNodes.add(v);
      this.boundedDijkstra([v]);
    }
    // Non-tree edge weight increase requires no action
  }

  boundedDijkstra(startNodes) {
    const queue = new FibonacciHeap();

    for (const node of startNodes) {
      queue.insert(node, this.distances[node]);
    }

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
      const { node: u, distance: du } = queue.extractMin();

      if (du > this.distances[u]) continue;

      for (const [v, weight] of this.graph.neighbors(u)) {
        const newDist = du + weight;
        if (newDist < this.distances[v]) {
          this.updateDistance(v, newDist, u);
          queue.insert(v, newDist);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  updateDistance(node, newDistance, parent) {
    this.distances[node] = newDistance;
    this.parents[node] = parent;
    this.affectedNodes.add(node);
  }

  propagateDecrease(startNode) {
    const queue = new FibonacciHeap();
    queue.insert(startNode, this.distances[startNode]);

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
      const { node: u } = queue.extractMin();

      for (const [v, weight] of this.graph.neighbors(u)) {
        if (this.parents[v] === u) {
          const newDist = this.distances[u] + weight;
          if (newDist < this.distances[v]) {
            this.updateDistance(v, newDist, u);
            queue.insert(v, newDist);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  initializeSSSP() {
    // Standard Dijkstra implementation
    this.distances[this.source] = 0;
    const heap = new FibonacciHeap();
    heap.insert(this.source, 0);

    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
      const { node: u, distance: du } = heap.extractMin();

      if (du > this.distances[u]) continue;

      for (const [v, weight] of this.graph.neighbors(u)) {
        const newDist = du + weight;
        if (newDist < this.distances[v]) {
          this.distances[v] = newDist;
          this.parents[v] = u;
          heap.insert(v, newDist);
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This implementation demonstrates the core principles of dynamic SSSP maintenance with efficient localized updates and amortized performance guarantees.

